# Fuzz corpus files are byte-exact test inputs, not text.
#
# Several seeds carry deliberate control bytes -- crlf_in_authority is
# "http://example.com\r\nHost: evil", and its whole point is the CR. Under
# core.autocrlf=true, which this repo relies on for normal source files, git
# normalises CRLF to LF on commit. The CR is destroyed in the blob, and a Linux
# CI runner checks the blob out verbatim, so the seed silently arrives without
# the byte it was written to carry.
#
# It is worse than a no-op seed: the file still reads as a two-line host
# injection, so it looks like coverage of a case it no longer exercises.
#
# `-text` turns off end-of-line conversion for these paths entirely, so the
# blob holds exactly the bytes the harness was fuzzed against, on every
# platform and in every checkout.
fuzzing/corpus/** -text
